      title: "The Farmers' Revolt",
      body: `## Hard times on the farm

In the decades after the Civil War, American farmers produced more than ever—and earned less. Production of wheat, corn, and cotton soared as new lands came under the plow and new machines multiplied what a family could grow. But prices fell. Cotton, which had sold for over 15 cents a pound in the early 1870s, fell to around 7 cents by the mid-1890s, and lower still by 1898; wheat fell from over a dollar a bushel to about 50 cents. The general deflation of the late nineteenth century, as the money supply failed to keep pace with economic growth, made every debt harder to repay: a farmer who borrowed when wheat sold for a dollar had to grow twice as much to repay the same loan when it sold for fifty cents.

Farmers blamed their troubles on forces beyond their control. **Railroads**, which often had a monopoly on transportation in a region, charged high rates, especially for short hauls, and gave rebates to large shippers. Grain elevator companies and merchants paid low prices and graded crops unfairly. Banks and mortgage companies, often in the East, charged high interest. In the South, as you saw in AMH 210, the **crop-lien system** trapped farmers, white and Black, in debt to merchants. And the federal government's commitment to the gold standard, which kept money scarce, seemed to favor creditors over debtors.

## The Grange

Farmers organized. In 1867 Oliver Kelley, a Department of Agriculture clerk, founded the Patrons of Husbandry, known as the **Grange**, as a social and educational organization for isolated farm families, open to women as well as men. In the 1870s it grew to some 800,000 members and turned to economic and political action. Granges organized cooperative stores and grain elevators, and pressed state legislatures in the Midwest to pass "**Granger laws**" regulating railroad and warehouse rates. In **Munn v. Illinois** (1877), the Supreme Court upheld Illinois's regulation of grain elevators, declaring that businesses "clothed with a public interest" could be regulated by the states. But in the *Wabash* case of 1886, the Court held that states could not regulate interstate railroad rates, leading Congress to pass the Interstate Commerce Act of 1887.

## The Farmers' Alliances

In the late 1870s and 1880s, a more ambitious movement arose: the **Farmers' Alliances**. Beginning in Texas, the Southern Farmers' Alliance spread rapidly across the South and West, claiming over a million members by 1890. A separate Northwestern Alliance organized farmers on the Plains. Excluded from the white Southern Alliance, Black farmers organized the **Colored Farmers' National Alliance**, which claimed as many as a million members, though the real number was probably much smaller.

The Alliances sent lecturers across the countryside to educate farmers about economics and politics. They organized cooperatives to buy supplies and market crops collectively, bypassing merchants and middlemen. Many cooperatives failed, for lack of capital and because banks and merchants refused to deal with them. The Texas Alliance leader **Charles Macune** devised an ingenious solution, the **subtreasury plan**: the federal government would build warehouses where farmers could store their crops and receive low-interest loans of paper money, up to 80 percent of the crop's value, until prices rose. The plan would have expanded the money supply and freed farmers from the crop lien. It was too radical for either major party.

## Movement culture

The historian **Lawrence Goodwyn**, in *The Populist Moment* (1978), argued that the Alliances created a democratic "**movement culture**": through lectures, encampments, newspapers, and cooperatives, ordinary farmers learned to analyze their situation, to see themselves as part of a larger struggle, and to believe they could change the system. Farm women played an important role, as lecturers, writers, and organizers. **Mary Elizabeth Lease**, a Kansas lawyer and orator, became famous for her fiery speeches against Wall Street; the advice often attributed to her, that farmers should "raise less corn and more hell," may have been a reporter's invention, but it captured the mood.

## Toward a party

At first, the Alliances tried to work through the existing parties, endorsing candidates who supported their demands. In 1890, Alliance-backed candidates won control of several state legislatures and elected dozens of congressmen. But Alliance leaders found that politicians elected with their support often abandoned their program once in office. In the South, the Democratic Party, the party of white supremacy, would not accept the subtreasury. In the West, Republicans were tied to Eastern business. By 1891 and 1892, many Alliance members concluded that they needed a party of their own. The decision was not easy. In the South, leaving the Democratic Party meant breaking with the party of white supremacy and of the Confederate dead, and risking social ostracism, economic retaliation, and violence. In the West, it meant breaking with the party of the Union. That so many farmers made the break is a measure of their desperation.

> **Pause and consider.** Why did deflation hurt farmers so badly? Who benefited from it?`,
