      title: "The Battle of 1896",
      body: `## The silver crusade

By 1896, the money question had become the dominant issue in American politics. Supporters of **free silver** argued that the unlimited coinage of silver would expand the money supply, raise prices, ease the burden of debt, and break the power of Eastern bankers. Silver mine owners in the West, who would profit directly, financed much of the campaign. A popular pamphlet, *Coin's Financial School* (1894) by William Harvey, explained the case for silver in simple terms and sold hundreds of thousands of copies. Defenders of the **gold standard** argued that it was the foundation of sound money and national credit; abandoning it, they warned, would bring inflation, destroy savings, and cause economic chaos.

## The Cross of Gold

At the Democratic convention in Chicago in July 1896, the silver forces took control of the party from Cleveland's Gold Democrats. On July 9, during the debate over the platform, a thirty-six-year-old former congressman from Nebraska, **William Jennings Bryan**, rose to speak. Bryan was a devout evangelical Christian and a spellbinding orator. He spoke for the producers of the West and South against the financiers of the East. "Burn down your cities and leave our farms," he said, "and your cities will spring up again as if by magic; but destroy our farms and the grass will grow in the streets of every city in the country." He closed with a famous image: "You shall not press down upon the brow of labor this crown of thorns; you shall not crucify mankind upon a cross of gold." As he spoke the last words, he spread his arms as if on a cross. The convention erupted. The next day, the Democrats nominated him for president. Gold Democrats walked out and nominated their own candidate.

## The Populist dilemma

Bryan's nomination put the Populists in an agonizing position. The Democrats had adopted their most popular demand, free silver, but ignored the rest of the Omaha Platform. If the Populists nominated their own candidate, they would split the silver vote and ensure the election of the Republican. If they endorsed Bryan, they would lose their independence. At their convention in St. Louis, they chose to nominate Bryan for president, but with their own vice-presidential candidate, Tom Watson, rather than the Democrats' nominee, a wealthy Maine banker and shipbuilder. The Populists were absorbed into the Democratic campaign, and never recovered.

## McKinley and Hanna

The Republicans nominated **William McKinley**, the governor of Ohio and author of the McKinley Tariff, on a platform supporting the gold standard and protective tariffs. His campaign was managed by **Mark Hanna**, a Cleveland industrialist, who raised an unprecedented campaign fund from corporations and banks—at least $3.5 million, and perhaps much more, against the Democrats' few hundred thousand. Hanna assessed banks and corporations a share of their assets. The money paid for millions of pamphlets, hundreds of speakers, and a vast organization.

McKinley campaigned from his front porch in Canton, Ohio, where delegations arrived by train, at reduced fares arranged by the railroads, to hear him speak. Bryan, by contrast, broke with tradition and campaigned across the country, traveling some 18,000 miles and making hundreds of speeches to millions of people—the first modern whistle-stop campaign.

Republicans warned that a Bryan victory would bring economic ruin. Employers told workers that factories would close if Bryan won. Republican newspapers portrayed Bryan as a dangerous radical, an anarchist, even a madman.

## The result

McKinley won decisively, with 51 percent of the popular vote and 271 electoral votes to Bryan's 176. Bryan swept the South and much of the West, but McKinley carried the populous Northeast and Midwest, including the industrial cities, where workers feared that free silver would raise prices without raising wages, and where Bryan's evangelical, rural appeal fell flat among Catholic and immigrant voters. Turnout was very high, nearly 80 percent.

## Realignment

The election of 1896 was one of the most important in American history. It ended the political stalemate of the Gilded Age and inaugurated a long period of Republican dominance: the Republicans would control the presidency for all but eight of the next thirty-six years, until 1932. The Democrats became the party of the South and, under Bryan, of agrarian protest; the Republicans the party of business, the industrial North, and the urban middle class. Political scientists call such a durable shift a "critical election" or "realignment."

The People's Party faded away. But many of its demands—the graduated income tax, the direct election of senators, the secret ballot, the initiative and referendum, government regulation of railroads, and a flexible currency—would be enacted by the Progressives over the next two decades.

## The money question settled

Ironically, the money question was resolved not by politics but by geology and chemistry. Major gold discoveries in South Africa, Australia, and the Klondike in Canada's Yukon, along with a new cyanide process for extracting gold from low-grade ore, increased the world's gold supply in the late 1890s. The money supply expanded, prices rose, and farm prosperity returned. The Gold Standard Act of 1900 made gold the sole standard of value. The years from about 1900 to 1914 would be remembered by farmers as a golden age.

## A fairy tale?

In 1964, a teacher named Henry Littlefield suggested that L. Frank Baum's *The Wonderful Wizard of Oz* (1900) was an allegory of Populism: the Scarecrow as the farmer, the Tin Woodman as the industrial worker, the Cowardly Lion as Bryan, the yellow brick road as the gold standard, and Dorothy's silver shoes (ruby slippers in the 1939 film) as free silver. The interpretation became popular in classrooms. Most historians now doubt that Baum intended any such allegory, and he himself never claimed it. The episode is a useful lesson in how historians can find patterns that are not there.

> **Pause and consider.** Why did Bryan's appeal fail to win urban workers, whom the Populists had hoped to unite with farmers?`,
