      title: "Theodore Roosevelt and the Square Deal",
      body: `## The accidental president

On September 6, 1901, President McKinley was shot by an anarchist, Leon Czolgosz, at the Pan-American Exposition in Buffalo; he died eight days later. His vice president, **Theodore Roosevelt**, became, at forty-two, the youngest president in American history. Mark Hanna, who had opposed Roosevelt's nomination for vice president, exclaimed: "Now look, that damned cowboy is President of the United States."

Roosevelt was born in 1858 to a wealthy New York family. A sickly, asthmatic child, he built up his body through strenuous exercise and adopted a lifelong cult of "the strenuous life." He graduated from Harvard, wrote history, ranched in the Dakota Badlands after the deaths of his wife and mother on the same day in 1884, served as a reforming New York City police commissioner and assistant secretary of the navy, led the "Rough Riders" in the Spanish-American War, and became governor of New York. New York's Republican bosses, eager to be rid of him, had arranged his nomination for vice president in 1900.

## The bully pulpit

Roosevelt transformed the presidency. He believed that the president should be a "steward of the people," entitled to do anything the Constitution did not forbid. He used the presidency as a "**bully pulpit**" (in the slang of the day, "bully" meant excellent) to rally public opinion. He was the first president to treat the press as a partner, providing a room for reporters in the White House. He sought to make the federal government a broker among competing interests—capital, labor, consumers—rather than a servant of any one of them. He called his program the "**Square Deal**."

## The trusts

Roosevelt did not oppose big business as such. He distinguished between "good trusts," which were efficient and served the public, and "bad trusts," which abused their power. But he insisted that the federal government, not the corporations, must be supreme. In 1902 his attorney general filed suit under the long-dormant Sherman Antitrust Act against the **Northern Securities Company**, a railroad holding company organized by J. P. Morgan, James J. Hill, and E. H. Harriman to control the railroads of the Northwest. Morgan went to the White House and suggested that if they had done anything wrong, "send your man to my man and they can fix it up." Roosevelt refused. In 1904 the Supreme Court ordered the company dissolved. Roosevelt became known as a "**trustbuster**," though he brought fewer antitrust suits than his successor, Taft.

## The coal strike

In 1902, some 150,000 anthracite coal miners in Pennsylvania, members of the United Mine Workers led by John Mitchell, went on strike for higher wages, shorter hours, and union recognition. The mine owners refused to negotiate; their spokesman, George Baer, declared that the rights of laborers would be protected "not by the labor agitators, but by the Christian men to whom God in His infinite wisdom has given the control of the property interests of the country." As winter approached and coal supplies dwindled, Roosevelt intervened, calling both sides to the White House—the first time a president had intervened in a labor dispute as a neutral mediator rather than on the side of employers. When the owners still refused, he threatened to have the army seize and operate the mines. They agreed to arbitration, and the miners won a 10 percent raise and a nine-hour day, though not union recognition.

## Regulation

After his election in his own right in 1904, Roosevelt pressed for regulation. The **Hepburn Act** of 1906 gave the Interstate Commerce Commission power to set maximum railroad rates. The **Meat Inspection Act** and **Pure Food and Drug Act**, passed the same year after the uproar over *The Jungle*, established federal inspection of meatpacking and banned the sale of adulterated or mislabeled food and medicines, laying the foundation for the Food and Drug Administration.

## Conservation

Roosevelt's most enduring legacy was **conservation**. An ardent outdoorsman and naturalist, he worked with **Gifford Pinchot**, the first chief of the U.S. Forest Service, to protect natural resources from reckless exploitation. Roosevelt set aside some 230 million acres of public land as national forests, national monuments, wildlife refuges, and parks. Under the **Antiquities Act** of 1906, he proclaimed eighteen national monuments, including the Grand Canyon. Pinchot and Roosevelt believed in "wise use": managing resources scientifically for long-term economic benefit. **John Muir**, the founder of the Sierra Club, by contrast, believed in preservation: protecting wilderness for its own sake. The two views collided over the proposal to dam the Hetch Hetchy Valley in Yosemite to supply water to San Francisco; the dam, approved in 1913, was a defeat for Muir.

## Race

Roosevelt's record on race was mixed. He invited Booker T. Washington to dine at the White House in 1901, provoking a storm of racist outrage in the South. But in 1906, after a shooting in **Brownsville**, Texas, was blamed, on flimsy evidence, on Black soldiers stationed there, Roosevelt dishonorably discharged 167 Black soldiers of the 25th Infantry without trial, among them men with decades of honorable service. In 1972 the army reversed the discharges; only one of the soldiers was still alive.

## Taft

Roosevelt chose his secretary of war, **William Howard Taft**, as his successor, and Taft won the presidency in 1908 against Bryan. Taft was a conservative lawyer who lacked Roosevelt's political skills. He brought about ninety antitrust suits, twice as many as Roosevelt, including the case that broke up Standard Oil in 1911. But he alienated Progressives by signing the Payne-Aldrich Tariff of 1909, which barely lowered rates, and by firing Pinchot in 1910 after Pinchot publicly accused Taft's secretary of the interior, Richard Ballinger, of favoring corporate interests in Alaska.

## The New Nationalism

Returning from an African safari in 1910, Roosevelt found the Republican Party split between Taft's conservatives and Progressive "Insurgents." In a speech at Osawatomie, Kansas, in August 1910, he proclaimed a "**New Nationalism**": a powerful federal government that would regulate business in the public interest, protect workers, and promote social justice. "The true friend of property, the true conservative, is he who insists that property shall be the servant and not the master of the commonwealth," he declared. The stage was set for a confrontation with Taft.

> **Pause and consider.** Roosevelt saw the federal government as a broker among capital, labor, and consumers. How did that differ from the government's role in the Pullman strike?`,
