      body: `## The Bull Moose

In 1912 Roosevelt challenged Taft for the Republican nomination. He won most of the primaries held that year—the first year presidential primaries played a significant role—but Taft controlled the party machinery and the convention, which renominated him. Roosevelt's supporters walked out and formed the **Progressive Party**. When a reporter asked how he felt, Roosevelt replied that he felt "as strong as a bull moose," and the party became known as the Bull Moose Party. Its platform called for a minimum wage for women, an eight-hour day, social insurance against sickness, unemployment, and old age, the prohibition of child labor, woman suffrage, and stronger regulation of corporations. Jane Addams seconded Roosevelt's nomination. The convention sang "Onward, Christian Soldiers." But it refused to seat Black delegates from the South, as Roosevelt sought white Southern support.

In October 1912, in Milwaukee, Roosevelt was shot in the chest by a would-be assassin. The bullet was slowed by his folded speech and his steel eyeglass case. He insisted on delivering his speech, about ninety minutes long, before going to the hospital. "It takes more than that to kill a Bull Moose," he told the crowd.

## Wilson and the New Freedom

The Democrats nominated **Woodrow Wilson**, the governor of New Jersey and former president of Princeton University. The son of a Presbyterian minister, Wilson had grown up in Georgia and South Carolina during the Civil War and Reconstruction. As governor, he had won a reputation as a reformer by breaking with the state's Democratic machine.

Wilson offered an alternative to Roosevelt's New Nationalism, which he called the **New Freedom**. Influenced by Louis Brandeis, who had written of "the curse of bigness," Wilson argued that big business was not inevitable or efficient, but the product of special privilege and financial manipulation. Rather than accept trusts and regulate them, as Roosevelt proposed, government should break them up and restore competition, opening opportunity to small businessmen and entrepreneurs. Wilson warned that Roosevelt's plan for a powerful regulatory state would lead to a partnership between big government and big business that would stifle individual initiative.

## Debs

The election also featured **Eugene V. Debs**, the Socialist Party candidate, whom you met in Lesson I. The Socialist Party, founded in 1901, had grown rapidly, electing over a thousand local officials, including mayors of Milwaukee, Berkeley, and Schenectady. Debs called for public ownership of major industries. He won about 900,000 votes, 6 percent of the total, the highest share ever for a Socialist presidential candidate.

## The election

With the Republicans divided, Wilson won easily, with 435 electoral votes, though only about 42 percent of the popular vote. Roosevelt finished second with 27 percent, and Taft third with 23 percent—the only time a sitting president has finished third. Together, the candidates who called themselves Progressive—Wilson and Roosevelt—won about 70 percent of the vote, and with Debs, three-quarters of the electorate had voted for some form of reform. The Progressive Party itself did not long survive; it depended on Roosevelt's personality, and most of its members drifted back to the Republicans by 1916. But many of its proposals, from social insurance to the minimum wage, would return in the New Deal.

## Wilson's reforms

Wilson proved to be a highly effective legislative leader. He revived the practice, abandoned since Jefferson, of delivering his messages to Congress in person, and he worked closely with Democratic leaders. In his first term, Congress enacted a remarkable series of reforms:

- The **Underwood Tariff** of 1913 lowered tariff rates substantially for the first time since the Civil War. To make up the lost revenue, it imposed a graduated **income tax**, made possible by the **Sixteenth Amendment**, ratified in February 1913. At first the tax applied only to the wealthiest few percent of Americans.
- The **Federal Reserve Act** of 1913 created a system of twelve regional Federal Reserve Banks, supervised by a Federal Reserve Board in Washington, to provide a flexible currency and serve as lenders of last resort. It was the most important banking reform since the Civil War, and it remains the foundation of the American monetary system.
- The **Federal Trade Commission Act** of 1914 created a commission to investigate and prevent "unfair methods of competition."
- The **Clayton Antitrust Act** of 1914 strengthened the Sherman Act, prohibiting specific practices such as price discrimination and interlocking directorates, and declared that "the labor of a human being is not a commodity or article of commerce," seeming to exempt unions from antitrust prosecution. Samuel Gompers hailed it as labor's "Magna Carta," though the courts would interpret it narrowly.

In 1916, seeking Progressive votes for reelection, Wilson moved toward Roosevelt's New Nationalism. He appointed Brandeis to the Supreme Court, the first Jewish justice, over fierce opposition. He signed the Keating-Owen Act banning goods made by child labor from interstate commerce (the Supreme Court struck it down in 1918), the Adamson Act establishing an eight-hour day for railroad workers, and laws providing workers' compensation for federal employees and farm credit.

## Wilson and race

Wilson's Progressivism did not extend to Black Americans. His administration, dominated by Southern Democrats, introduced segregation into federal departments that had been integrated since Reconstruction, segregating offices, lunchrooms, and restrooms, and demoting or firing Black civil servants. When the Black newspaper editor **William Monroe Trotter**, who had supported Wilson in 1912, protested in a White House meeting in November 1914, Wilson ordered him out, saying that segregation was in the interest of both races. In 1915 Wilson screened D. W. Griffith's *The Birth of a Nation*, which glorified the Ku Klux Klan, at the White House. In recent years, Princeton University removed Wilson's name from its school of public and international affairs in recognition of his racism.

> **Pause and consider.** Roosevelt proposed to regulate big business; Wilson proposed to break it up. Which approach prevailed in practice?`,
